🚽 Test case: issue #271 -- `smol % 0` had no zero-divisor guard at all
🚽 and crashed the interpreter with SIGFPE.
🚽
🚽 handle_binary_operation()'s OP_MOD branch guarded VAR_INT, and OP_DIVIDE
🚽 guarded both VAR_INT and VAR_SHORT -- but OP_MOD's VAR_SHORT arm went
🚽 straight to the modulo. So `smol / 0` reported "Division by zero"
🚽 cleanly while `smol % 0`, one operator away, took down the process.
🚽
🚽 The result is printed rather than discarded so this pins the fallback
🚽 value too, not just the message: an implementation that reported the
🚽 error and then left the slot uninitialized would still fail here.
🚽
🚽 Both the int and short forms are exercised, so the fixture also pins
🚽 that they agree -- reporting the same diagnostic and the same fallback
🚽 is the property that was missing, and one guard existing without the
🚽 other is exactly the shape of the bug.
skibidi main {
    rizz ia = 7;
    rizz ib = 0;
    yapping("rizz: %d", ia % ib);

    smol sa = 7;
    smol sb = 0;
    yapping("smol: %d", sa % sb);
    bussin 0;
}
